From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Clients delete, rename and reopen their own files

Stage 2 of Stained Glass ADR 0013 (multi-user debt D14). Patch 0014 moved
open() into the client, so the kernel checks the real user's rights; but the
shared machine-level server, running as SYSTEM, still performed every
deletion, rename and hard link by path -- and refused reopening outright for
other users. With this patch the server does none of those with its own
rights for anyone but its own Unix account.

  - Deletion (delete-on-close, FileDispositionInformation, DeleteFile). The
    server still decides *when*: the last handle to the file closing. It
    records which Unix user asked for the deletion; if that is not its own
    account, it does not unlink, and when a close_handle request makes the
    deletion due and the closing client runs as the user who asked, the reply
    lists the file (device, inode, path) and the client unlinks it itself,
    after checking device and inode as the server does. If the last handle is
    closed by anyone else -- another user, SYSTEM, or process exit -- the file
    stays: nobody else's rights may be used.
  - Rename and hard link (FileRenameInformation, FileLinkInformation). A new
    stage field on set_fd_name_info: stage 1 runs all the server's checks
    (sharing, replace, directories) and performs it only for the server's own
    account; for anyone else it answers client_performs, the client renames or
    links with its own rights -- mirroring set_fd_name(): target removal for
    links and directories, RENAME_NOREPLACE where replacing is not allowed,
    and the .exe/.com executable bits -- and stage 2 commits the fd's new
    names (the Unix name read back from the fd). The old one-shot request
    (stage 0) is refused to other users' clients.
  - Reopening (ReOpenFile, NtCreateFile with an empty name relative to a file
    handle). The client reopens the file through /proc/self/fd, so the kernel
    checks its rights on that inode, and sends the fd with open_file_object;
    the server checks it is the same file and adopts it.

Protocol: close_handle gains reply data, open_file_object an fd, and
set_fd_name_info a stage and a reply.

Tested: Wine's kernel32 file/directory/path/module and ntdll
file/directory/om/info conformance tests, as the server's own user, match the
unpatched baseline (the 4 todo_wine checks for STATUS_CANNOT_DELETE that 0014
made pass still pass). Run as a second Unix user against a shared server,
where every new path executes: kernel32:directory and ntdll:directory pass;
kernel32:file fails only the SE_MANAGE_VOLUME_NAME privilege check (that user
is not an administrator); kernel32:path fails only copying into a directory
the user cannot write; ntdll:file fails only two I/O completion tests, because
the server cannot signal another user's threads (tgkill EPERM) -- a separate,
pre-existing limitation of the shared server, not of this patch.

